(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-3-male-reproductive-quiz-version-4"]={"title":"Male Reproductive System — Anatomy Exam 3 (Set 4)","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"The mesonephric duct and diaphragmatic ligament appear in early diagrams of testicular descent alongside the developing gonads near which organs?","choices":["The liver and spleen","The developing lungs","The developing kidneys","The urinary bladder only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Early diagrams of testicular descent show the gonads, mesonephric duct, and diaphragmatic ligament positioned near the developing kidneys, reflecting the testes' origin high in the posterior abdominal wall. — Slide 12 (The Descent of the Testes)"},{"q":"As the testes descend, which structures remain bundled with them within the spermatic cords?","choices":["The bulbo-urethral glands at the penile base","Only the single ureter, coursing separately","The prostate and seminal glands, formed in place","Ductus deferens, blood vessels, lymphatics, and nerves"],"answer":3,"exp":"As the testes descend, the ductus deferens, blood vessels (testicular and deferential arteries, pampiniform plexus), lymphatics, and nerves all remain bundled together to form the spermatic cords. — Slide 9 (Descent of the Testes)"},{"q":"The serous membrane that comes to line each scrotal cavity as the testis descends is the:","choices":["Dartos fascia","Tunica albuginea","Tunica vaginalis","Peritoneum proper"],"answer":2,"exp":"The tunica vaginalis is the serous membrane, derived from the processus vaginalis, that comes to line each scrotal cavity as the testis descends and reduces friction around it. — Slides 13, 16 (tunica vaginalis)"},{"q":"The two chambers of the scrotum are separated externally by a ridge of tissue called the:","choices":["Inguinal ligament","Linea alba","Median umbilical ligament","Raphe of the scrotum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The raphe of the scrotum is the external ridge of tissue marking the separation between the two scrotal chambers, running from the anus across the scrotum and onto the penis. — Slide 16 (Scrotum)"},{"q":"The bulbospongiosus muscle is associated with which structure at the base of the penis?","choices":["The prostate gland near the bladder","The corpora cavernosa exclusively","The bulb of the penis / spongy urethra","The epididymis atop each testis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The bulbospongiosus muscle overlies and surrounds the bulb of the penis and spongy urethra, and its contraction (with the ischiocavernosus) helps propel semen during ejaculation. — Slides 49, 67 (Bulbospongiosus / ejaculation)"},{"q":"The scrotal septum internally corresponds to which external landmark?","choices":["The perineal body region","The pubic symphysis joint","The inguinal ligament","The raphe of the scrotum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The internal scrotal septum, which divides the scrotum into two separate cavities, corresponds externally to the visible raphe of the scrotum. — Slides 15, 16 (Scrotal septum / raphe)"},{"q":"The external pudendal artery, contributing to scrotal supply, arises from which vessel?","choices":["External iliac artery","Internal iliac artery","Femoral artery","Abdominal aorta"],"answer":2,"exp":"The external pudendal artery, which helps supply the scrotum, is a branch of the femoral artery. — Slide 21 (scrotal blood supply)"},{"q":"The inferior epigastric artery, which contributes to scrotal vascularity, branches from the:","choices":["External iliac artery","Femoral artery","Internal iliac artery","Renal artery"],"answer":0,"exp":"The inferior epigastric artery, which contributes to scrotal vascularity, is a branch of the external iliac artery. — Slide 21 (scrotal blood supply)"},{"q":"The spermatic cord contains which two arteries supplying the testis and ductus deferens?","choices":["Testicular artery and deferential artery","Renal artery and gonadal vein","Internal pudendal and femoral arteries","Inferior epigastric and obturator arteries"],"answer":0,"exp":"The spermatic cord carries the testicular artery (supplying the testis) and the deferential artery (supplying the ductus deferens), along with the pampiniform plexus and autonomic nerves. — Slides 14–15 (The Spermatic Cords)"},{"q":"The scrotum receives sensory and motor supply from all of the following EXCEPT the:","choices":["Pudendal nerve","Ilioinguinal nerve","Genitofemoral nerve","Vagus nerve"],"answer":3,"exp":"The scrotum is supplied by the pudendal, ilioinguinal, and genitofemoral nerves along with the hypogastric plexus — the vagus nerve is not among its sources. — Slide 22 (scrotal nerve supply)"},{"q":"During erection, the smooth muscle in the penile arterial walls does what in response to parasympathetic activity?","choices":["Relaxes, dilating the arteries","Contracts, constricting the arteries","Remains unchanged","Undergoes fibrosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Parasympathetic activity relaxes the smooth muscle in the walls of the penile arteries, dilating them so blood engorges the erectile tissue and produces erection. — Slide 66 (Erection of the penis)"},{"q":"The genitofemoral nerve, which supplies the scrotum, is also a named component of which structure?","choices":["The pampiniform plexus","The spermatic cord","The ejaculatory duct","The prostatic plexus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The genitofemoral nerve, in addition to supplying the scrotum, is listed among the structures running within the spermatic cord. — Slides 14–15, 22 (spermatic cord / scrotal nerves)"},{"q":"Which accessory gland produces a weakly acidic secretion containing seminalplasmin, an antibiotic thought to help prevent UTIs?","choices":["Prostate","Seminal gland","Bulbo-urethral gland","Epididymis"],"answer":0,"exp":"The prostate secretes a weakly acidic fluid (about 20–30% of semen volume) containing seminalplasmin, an antibiotic believed to help prevent urinary tract infections in males. — Slide 56 (The Prostate)"},{"q":"Each seminiferous tubule is approximately how long?","choices":["About 8 mm","About 8 cm","About 80 cm","About 8 meters"],"answer":2,"exp":"Each tightly coiled seminiferous tubule is approximately 80 cm long, which allows an enormous total length of tubule to be packed into the testis. — Slide 25 (Histology of the Testes)"},{"q":"The head of the epididymis receives spermatozoa via which structures?","choices":["The ejaculatory duct","The efferent ductules","The straight tubules directly","The ampulla"],"answer":1,"exp":"The head of the epididymis receives spermatozoa via the efferent ductules, which carry sperm from the rete testis into the epididymis. — Slide 42 (The Epididymis)"},{"q":"The blood-testis barrier, maintained by nurse cells, functions primarily to:","choices":["Trigger ejaculation through peristaltic smooth-muscle contraction","Actively concentrate calcium ions within the seminiferous tubular fluid","Produce testosterone directly within the tubular epithelium","Isolate seminiferous tubules from circulation to prevent autoimmune destruction of sperm"],"answer":3,"exp":"The blood-testis barrier, maintained by tight junctions between nurse (Sertoli) cells, isolates the seminiferous tubules from the general circulation to prevent the immune system from recognizing developing sperm as foreign and destroying them. — Slide 36 (Nurse Cells)"},{"q":"The three cylindrical erectile columns of the penis are:","choices":["Two corpora spongiosa and one bulb","Three corpora cavernosa","One corpus cavernosum and two corpora spongiosa","Two corpora cavernosa and one corpus spongiosum"],"answer":3,"exp":"The penis contains three columns of erectile tissue: two dorsal corpora cavernosa and one ventral corpus spongiosum, which surrounds the spongy urethra. — Slide 61 (The Penis)"},{"q":"The root of the penis attaches its base to which bony structures?","choices":["The sacrum","The pubic symphysis only","The rami of the ischia","The iliac crests"],"answer":2,"exp":"The root of the penis anchors its base to the rami of the ischia, where each corpus cavernosum forms a crus that attaches to the ischial ramus. — Slides 60, 62 (The Penis)"},{"q":"After leaving the tail of the epididymis, the ductus deferens ascends into the abdomen through which passage?","choices":["The inguinal canal","The femoral canal","The obturator canal","The ureteric orifice"],"answer":0,"exp":"After leaving the tail of the epididymis, the ductus deferens ascends into the abdominal cavity by passing through the inguinal canal within the spermatic cord. — Slide 47 (The Ductus Deferens)"},{"q":"Which is the correct order of accessory-gland/duct contributions as semen is assembled toward ejaculation?","choices":["Ejaculatory duct → ampulla → spongy urethra → prostatic urethra","Spongy urethra → prostatic urethra → ejaculatory duct → ampulla","Prostatic urethra → ampulla → ejaculatory duct → spongy urethra","Ductus deferens/ampulla → ejaculatory duct → prostatic urethra → spongy urethra"],"answer":3,"exp":"Semen is assembled in order from the ductus deferens/ampulla into the ejaculatory duct, which empties into the prostatic urethra, continuing through the membranous urethra into the spongy urethra toward ejaculation. — Slides 48, 51 (Ductus Deferens / Urethra)"},{"q":"Roughly what volume of semen is released in a typical ejaculation?","choices":["20 to 50 mL","2 to 5 mL","0.1 to 0.5 mL","10 to 15 mL"],"answer":1,"exp":"A typical ejaculation releases about 2 to 5 mL of semen, containing roughly 20–100 million sperm per mL. — Slide 59 (Semen)"},{"q":"During emission, sperm are mixed with glandular secretions by peristaltic contractions of smooth muscle in the ductus deferens, seminal glands, prostate, and:","choices":["Corpora cavernosa","Epididymal head only","Bulbo-urethral glands","Rete testis network"],"answer":2,"exp":"During emission, sperm are mixed with glandular secretions by peristaltic smooth-muscle contractions in the ductus deferens, seminal glands, prostate, and bulbo-urethral glands. — Slide 67 (Semen release and ejaculation)"},{"q":"The spongy urethra is also known by which name?","choices":["Prostatic urethra","Penile urethra","Membranous urethra","Cavernous canal"],"answer":1,"exp":"The spongy urethra, which passes through the penis within the corpus spongiosum, is also called the penile urethra. — Slide 51 (The Urethra)"},{"q":"The bulbo-urethral glands empty their secretions into which portion of the urethra, just distal to the body wall?","choices":["The internal urethral orifice","The prostatic urethra","The membranous urethra","The spongy urethra"],"answer":3,"exp":"The bulbo-urethral glands empty their secretions into the spongy urethra, just distal to the body wall at the end of the membranous urethra. — Slide 57 (The Bulbo-urethral Glands)"},{"q":"The external urethral orifice is surrounded by which expanded distal region of the penis?","choices":["The crus of the penis","The bulb of the penis","The glans penis","The neck of the glans"],"answer":2,"exp":"The glans penis, the expanded distal end of the penis formed by the corpus spongiosum, surrounds and encloses the external urethral orifice. — Slide 60 (The Penis)"},{"q":"The internal urethral orifice marks the junction between the urinary bladder and which urethral segment?","choices":["The membranous urethra","The prostatic urethra","The spongy urethra","The ejaculatory duct"],"answer":1,"exp":"The internal urethral orifice lies at the base of the bladder and marks its junction with the prostatic urethra, the first segment of the male urethra. — Slides 49, 51 (Urethra)"},{"q":"The prepuce (foreskin) attaches to which part of the penis?","choices":["The bulb of the penis","The neck of the glans","The crus of the penis","The root of the penis"],"answer":1,"exp":"The prepuce (foreskin) surrounds the tip of the penis and attaches at the neck of the glans, where preputial glands secrete the lubricating substance smegma. — Slide 63 (The Penis)"},{"q":"Spermatozoa typically take about how long to pass through the epididymis?","choices":["About 2 weeks","About 2 days","About 2 months","About 2 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Spermatozoa take about 2 weeks to pass through the epididymis, during which they are stored, monitored, and undergo further maturation. — Slide 44 (The Epididymis)"},{"q":"The dartos muscle differs from the cremaster in that the dartos is:","choices":["A skeletal muscle that elevates the testes","A superficial smooth muscle that wrinkles the scrotal skin","A perineal muscle active during ejaculation","A circular sphincter muscle of the urethra"],"answer":1,"exp":"The dartos is a superficial smooth muscle that contracts to wrinkle the scrotal skin, whereas the cremaster is a skeletal muscle that elevates the testes toward the body. — Slides 18–19 (Muscles of the scrotum)"},{"q":"The pampiniform plexus is thought to aid testicular function by:","choices":["Cooling arterial blood via countercurrent heat exchange","Producing testosterone within Leydig cells","Storing mature sperm in the epididymal tail","Secreting seminal fluid directly into the urethra"],"answer":0,"exp":"The pampiniform plexus, a network of veins surrounding the testicular artery in the spermatic cord, aids testicular function through countercurrent heat exchange, cooling incoming arterial blood before it reaches the testis. — Slides 14–15 (The Spermatic Cords)"}]};
